summary:
  The Qt-metadata latch fires on the FIRST title again, killing the fallback.

history:
  Issue #10 (2026-08-08, Fedora): Now Playing froze on the first track. The
  latch is one-way — once Qt hands over a single title, infoTimer stops itself
  and the only ways back are a station change or a bitrate fallback. Measured
  2026-08-09: Qt's ffmpeg backend emits metaDataChanged exactly once per load,
  so a stream that goes quiet leaves both sources dead.

target:
  package/contents/ui/main.qml

find:
              if (cleaned !== root._qtMetaFirstTitle && root._qtMetaFirstTitle !== "") {
                  root._qtMetaWorks = true;
                  infoTimer.stop();
                  fastRetryTimer.stop();
              }
              if (root._qtMetaFirstTitle === "") root._qtMetaFirstTitle = cleaned;

replace:
              if (true) {
                  root._qtMetaWorks = true;
                  infoTimer.stop();
                  fastRetryTimer.stop();
              }
              if (root._qtMetaFirstTitle === "") root._qtMetaFirstTitle = cleaned;

expect:
  Killed by tests/test_invariants.py::test_the_stuck_titles_holes_stay_closed —
  the two-title gate is the 2026-08-11 fix for exactly the freeze this mutant
  documents: latching on the FIRST title retires the polling fallback on the
  strength of a single hello, and a backend that never emits a mid-stream
  update leaves both sources dead. The invariant greps for the gate this
  mutant deletes. The replacement stays line-neutral so the size ratchet can
  never claim this kill.
